# Beat 8 of grpconf/synopsis.md — 2:00, inference.
#
# Payload: grpconf/stage/bobshark, 84 bytes, type unknown.
# Database: grpconf/stage/bobapp1.desc — 64 816 B, 41 files recovered from
# the OLDER binary sixty seconds earlier.  One version of the Routes API,
# so there is nothing here to be ambiguous between.  The tie lives in beat
# 10, under bobapp2.desc, and that is deliberate.
#
# Invocation:
#   protolens --descriptor-set $DESC1 -I $SRC1 \
#       --script grpconf/beats/infer.script $BOBSHARK
#
# node: lines below use SEARCH STRINGS, not positional paths.  A search
# string is matched against the rendered text of every node; the script
# lands on the first match.  A search that misses is a visible, recoverable
# miss; a positional path that misses silently lands on the wrong node.
# Re-pin by running:
#
#   protolens --descriptor-set $DESC1 -I $SRC1 \
#       --script grpconf/beats/infer.script $BOBSHARK script \
#     | grep 'error:'

title: One payload, 39 candidates

steps:
  - text: |
      No .proto files.  No type name.  One captured request, 84 bytes,
      and a schema database that was inside the executable two minutes
      ago.  Every type in it was scored against these bytes.

      Press space to hand navigation to this pane, then -> to step.
    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /

  - text: |
      The winner.  Thirty-nine points clear of the runner-up.

      Read the score breakdown in the status line: unknown: 1,
      non_canonical: 1.  Two of the four anomalies are counted before the
      file has been opened.  The tool is saying that the best type it has
      does not completely explain these bytes — and it is right.

      This is the first time protolens has been on screen.  The caret
      is a highlighted row.  j/k or arrow keys move it.  h folds, l
      unfolds, space hands navigation back to you.
    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /

  - text: |
      A scoring function sees wire shape: which field numbers occur,
      which wire types they carry, whether the lengths close, whether
      anything is out of range.  It does not see intent.

      Two types with the same field numbers and wire types are
      indistinguishable to it.  That is not a contrivance.  It is what
      a wire-shape score is entitled to conclude.  The ranking says:
      this is the best match I have.  Then it defers.
    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /

  - text: |
      The command line below is prefilled.  Press Enter to apply it,
      or Esc to pick a different type from the override pane by hand.

      Announce the choice out loud.  A flawless instantaneous demo
      reads as a recording; work shown is proof.
    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /
    command: override / --as google.maps.routing.v2.ComputeRoutesRequest

  - text: |
      Same bytes.  Now they have names.

      Nothing was re-parsed and nothing was re-read: the payload was
      always this shape.  The only thing that changed is which of the
      39 answers we are looking through.

      Unfold a submessage with l, fold back with h.
    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /

  - text: |
      Before we leave: press :quit.

      Beat 9 opens the log with the same database — the one that was
      inside the executable two minutes ago.
    node: /
